            "title": "no-obj-calls no longer reports non-callable Reflect",
            "severity": "medium",
            "file": "lib/rules/no-obj-calls.js",
            "line": 31,
            "explanation": "The rule now only checks `Math` and `JSON`, so a direct call to the ES2015 global `Reflect` object is missed even though `Reflect` has no [[Call]] method and `Reflect()` throws at runtime. This is a false negative for the rule's purpose of flagging calls to non-callable global objects.",
            "suggestion": "Include `Reflect` in the condition again, e.g. `if (name === \"Math\" || name === \"JSON\" || name === \"Reflect\")`.",
            "failureAfterChange": "Linting `var x = Reflect();` now produces no `no-obj-calls` error, but executing the code throws `TypeError: Reflect is not a function`.",
